{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790998317,"nonce":"yYGbImIeDl7NdKhkHBQoP5NrLig=","message":"Measured on the NUC (quiet, pinned to physical cores 0,2,4,6), ash at fc5c3ab + the tiny-callee inliner change, stock hl from ~/hashlink. Benchmark: FixedThreadPool(4), rounds of 4 jobs joined on a Lock, each job `work` sqrt iterations; per-round cost, three reps each.\n\n| engine | work=2000 serial | pooled | work=20000 serial | pooled |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| stock hl | 40-42 us | 28-29 us | 383-385 us | 174-189 us |\n| ash jit | 16-17 us | 19-21 us | 147-150 us | 94-100 us |\n| ash hybrid | 12-13 us | 21-25 us | 148-219 us | 90-106 us |\n| ash AOT | 13 us | 15-20 us | 111-114 us | 73-78 us |\n| ash interp | 393-403 us | 404-411 us | 3927-4136 us | 3973-4487 us |\n\nDispatch-and-join overhead per round of 4 (pooled minus serial/4) is ~15-20 us for ash's compiled modes and ~18 us for stock hl at the small size; ~60 us vs ~80 us at the large one. So ash's dispatch cost matches HashLink's on Linux; jobs of tens of microseconds simply do not pay for a pool on either. ASH_WORKERS=4 versus the default made no difference.\n\ninterp gets no parallelism at all (user time = wall time): by design, a thread body goes to an OS worker only when it is compiled (`RT_THREAD_COMPILED` in std/src/fiber.rs); an interpreted body runs on the main scheduler.\n\nThe main thread's Lock.wait waits on the scheduler endpoint's condvar (1 ms timeout), so wake-up is prompt; no polling loop.\n\nChimpMonk's 2-5x slowdown was measured on the Mac at load 30-190, where thread wake-up latency is the machine's. Stock hl does not run on M-series, so the Mac has no baseline; a quiet-Mac run of the same benchmark against these NUC numbers is the remaining check.\n\nBenchmark source: PoolBench.hx (usage `PoolBench \u003cworkers\u003e \u003crounds\u003e \u003cwork\u003e`), kept with this comment:\n\n    import sys.thread.FixedThreadPool;\n    import sys.thread.Lock;\n    \n    /**\n    \tDispatch-and-join cost of FixedThreadPool: `rounds` rounds of `workers`\n    \tjobs, each doing `work` iterations, joined on a Lock. Prints the\n    \tper-round cost and the serial cost of the same work.\n    \tUsage: PoolBench \u003cworkers\u003e \u003crounds\u003e \u003cwork\u003e\n    **/\n    class PoolBench {\n    \tstatic var sink = new haxe.ds.Vector\u003cFloat\u003e(64);\n    \n    \tstatic function job(slot:Int, work:Int):Void {\n    \t\tvar x = 0.0;\n    \t\tfor (i in 0...work)\n    \t\t\tx += Math.sqrt(i + slot);\n    \t\tsink[slot] = x;\n    \t}\n    \n    \tstatic function main() {\n    \t\tvar args = Sys.args();\n    \t\tvar workers = args.length \u003e 0 ? Std.parseInt(args[0]) : 4;\n    \t\tvar rounds = args.length \u003e 1 ? Std.parseInt(args[1]) : 2000;\n    \t\tvar work = args.length \u003e 2 ? Std.parseInt(args[2]) : 2000;\n    \t\tvar pool = new FixedThreadPool(workers);\n    \t\tvar done = new Lock();\n    \t\t// Warm up both paths so compile time stays out of the numbers.\n    \t\tfor (r in 0...50) {\n    \t\t\tfor (w in 0...workers)\n    \t\t\t\tpool.run(() -\u003e { job(w, work); done.release(); });\n    \t\t\tfor (_ in 0...workers)\n    \t\t\t\tdone.wait();\n    \t\t\tfor (w in 0...workers)\n    \t\t\t\tjob(w, work);\n    \t\t}\n    \t\tvar t0 = Sys.time();\n    \t\tfor (r in 0...rounds) {\n    \t\t\tfor (w in 0...workers)\n    \t\t\t\tpool.run(() -\u003e { job(w, work); done.release(); });\n    \t\t\tfor (_ in 0...workers)\n    \t\t\t\tdone.wait();\n    \t\t}\n    \t\tvar pooled = Sys.time() - t0;\n    \t\tt0 = Sys.time();\n    \t\tfor (r in 0...rounds)\n    \t\t\tfor (w in 0...workers)\n    \t\t\t\tjob(w, work);\n    \t\tvar serial = Sys.time() - t0;\n    \t\tSys.println('workers=$workers rounds=$rounds work=$work pooled=${Math.round(pooled * 1e6 / rounds)}us/round serial=${Math.round(serial * 1e6 / rounds)}us/round');\n    \t\tpool.shutdown();\n    \t}\n    }","files":null}]}